Macedonia made a referendum on changing the country's name
Voters will vote on Sunday whether to change their country’s name to ‘Norther Republic of Macedonia.’
A popular vote was established in an attempt to resolve decades of disagreements with neighboring Greece, which has its own territory called Macedonia.
Athens has long asserted that the name of its north-side country represents claims to its territory and has repeatedly objected to its offer of membership for the EU and NATO.
Macedonian President Gjorge Ivanov, an opponent of a referendum on the change of name, said he would ignore the vote.
However, supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argued that the name change was merely the price to pay to join EU and NATO unions.
