The Macedonian people made a referendum to change the country's name
The Macedonians made a referendum on Sunday to determine the change of the name to ‘North Macedonian republic’. An action to resolve disputes with Greece that has been going on for a decade in which Greece has prevented Macedonia from joining the European Union (EU) and the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO).
Greece has its own territory named Macedonia asserts that the name ‘Macedonia’ represents a claim over its territory and has veted to reject Macedonia’s participation in NATO and the EU.
The two kingdoms reached an agreement on the proposed name change in June but the nationalist opposition said the conversion lowered the identity of the Macedonian-majority, the majority occupied of Slavic.
President Gjorge Ivanov said he would not vote in the referendum and the boycott had cast doubt on whether the referendum would achieve a minimum of 50 per cent of the votes required so that the referendum was valid.
“Do you agree to join NATO and the EU with the acceptance of an agreement with Greece?”
Proponents of name change including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev said “Receiving a new name is worth a paid price to join the EU and NATO for Macedonia, one of the countries that emerged after the fall of Yugoslavia.”
��“I’m coming today to vote for the future of the country and for the young people in Macedonia so they can live freely under the European Union because that means our life is safer,” said Olivera Georgijevska, 79 at Skopje.
Although it is illegal, the MPs said they would abide by the results of the vote to make it final.
The change of name requires two-thirds majority in parliament.
The country’s election commission said there was no report on matters that did not follow the rules as of 1 p.m.
However, the turnout was 16 per cent compared to 34 per cent in a parliamentary election in 2016 after 66 per cent of registered voters cast their ballot papers.
“I’m here to vote because of my children’s. Our place in Europe,” said Gjose Tanevski, 62, a voter in Skopje’s capital city.”
Macedonian PM Zoran Zaev, his wife and their son Dushko cast a ballot paper in Macedonia for the change of the country's name which will pave the way for Macedonia to join NATO and the EU in Strumica, Macedonia on 30 September 2018.
In front of parliament in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, is setting up a small stage and placing seats in front of tents erected by those boycotting the referendum.
“We’d like to join NATO and the EU but we’d like to join it with pride and not this way,” said Kavadarkov.
“We’re the people of a poor country but we have dignity.”
If they don’t want to see us as Macedonian citizens, we can turn towards China and Russia and become part of Euro-Asia integration.””
Prime Minister Zaev said participation in NATO would provide enough investment to Macedonia where the unemployment rate exceeded 20 percent.
I believe a large majority will accept because more than 80 percent of our population elects the EU and NATO,” Zaev said after casting votes.
He said ‘yes’’’ is ‘confirmation of our future.’’
A vote was published on Monday by the Macedonian Institute for a Policy Review saying that between 30 and 43 percent of the voters would take part in a referendum - below the required amount.
Another votion, conducted by Macedonian TV Telma, found 57 percent of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
Of them, 70 percent said they would vote yes.
For a referendum, the number of voters who came must be 50 per cent plus one vote.
Failure in the referendum will be considered the first serious blow to pro-Western government policies since taking over in May last year.
