John Edward: Language skills are very important for global citizens
Scotland's private schools maintain a track record for academic excellence, and this has continued in 2018 with another set of excellent exam results, reinforced by individuals and collective success in sports, arts, music and other community efforts.
With more than 30,000 students throughout Scotland, the school, represented by the Scottish Private Schools Board (SCIS), strives to serve at the best level to their students and parents.
The aim of private schools is to prepare their students to pursue higher education, the careers of their choice, and their place as a global citizen.
As an educational sector they can and implement an earlier school curriculum, we see the modern language continues to be well-known and the subject of choice required within the school.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you speak to someone in his language, it will come into his head.
If you speak to him in his mother tongue, it will come into his heart."
This is a strong reminder that we can't just rely on English if we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
Out of this year’s exam results, we can see that the language is the top subject in the league table with the highest passing rates in private schools.
A total of 68 per cent of students who learn a foreign language get a higher grade A.
This data, collected from 74 SCIS school members, showed 72 percent of students scored higher A grades in Mandarin, while 72 per cent of them studied German, 69 percent of them learned French and 63 per cent studied Spanish also got an A.
This demonstrates that private schools in Scotland support a foreign language as an important skill that children and young people will undoubtedly need them in the future
The current language, as the subject of choice, is being held the same thing as STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the curriculum of private schools and elsewhere.
A survey by the UK's Occupational and Skills Commission in 2014 found the reasons employers were struggling to meet vacancies, 17 per cent linked to a lack of language skills.
That's why again and again, language skills are a must to prepare young people for their future careers.
With more prospective employment opportunities requiring languages, these skills are very important in this globalized world.
No matter which one's preferred career, if they learn a second language, they'll have a real advantage in the future by having lifelong skills like this.
Able to communicate directly with people from a foreign country will place someone who masters many languages automatically ahead of the competition.
According to YouGov's vote more than 4,000 UK adults in 2013, 75 percent couldn't speak in a foreign linguage well enough for a conversation, with French as a spoken language used only by double-digit percentage, 15 percent.
This is why investment in language teaching now is important for today's children.
Having a variety of languages, especially developing countries its economy, will equip children with better opportunities in finding meaningful jobs.
DiScotland, each school will be different in the language they learn.
Several schools will focus on more modern classical languages, while others will teach a very important language for the UK when looking forward in 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Whatever your child is interested in, there are often many languages to choose from from within private schools, with the teaching staff specializing in that division.
Scottish private schools are dedicated to providing a learning environment that will prepare children and provide them with the skills needed to succeed, whatever happens in the future.
Undoubtedly at the moment, in a global business environment, the language continues to be very important to the country's future, so this must be reflected in education.
Indeed, the modern language should be regarded as ‘international communication skills’.’
Private schools will continue to offer these options, diversity and excellence for young people in Scotland.
It's il Faut bien lee faire.
John Edward is the Director of the Council of Scottish Private Schools
