North Korea says "uncomposiable" they will disqualifill their armmours unconditionally
North Korea's foreign minister told the United Nations on Saturday that ongoing sanctions had caused their disbelief in the United States and it was unlikely that his country would release nuclear weapons voluntarily in that situation.
Ri Yong Ho told the world body's annual General Assembly, North Korea, had taken an "important goodwill step" last year, such as halting nuclear and missile tests, shutting down nuclear test sites and promising not to expand the use of nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
"However, we don't see any corresponding reactions from the U.S.," he said.
"Without any trust in the U.S., there will be no confidence in the security of our country and in such a situation, there is no way that we will disqualify our weapons first."
While Ri rebuked complaints against North Korea about Washington's "facing" a "facing" nucleur termination approach in which North Korea will be rewarded because it takes a tolerant step, his statement seems important because it did not reject voluntary rejection as Pyongyang had done in the past.
Ri was referring to a joint statement issued by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at the first summit between U.S. president and North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, when Kim promised to work for "the nuclear termination of the Korean peninsula" while Trump assured North Korea's security.
North Korea is trying to find an end for the 1950-53 Korean War, but the United States said that Pyongyang should give up its nuclear weapons first.
Washington also refuses to follow a recommendation to relax international sanctions against North Korea.
"The U.S. asserts "precitation" and raises stress levels in sanctions to forcibly achieve their goals, and even resists "the end-of-the-war declaration," Ri said.
"The perception that restrictions can weaken us is an empty dream of those who don't know us.
But the problem is a continuous restriction that worsens our distrust."
Ri did not mention the plans for the second summit between Kim and Trump which was highlighted by the U.S. leader during a United Nations conference earlier this week.
The minister mentioned only three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Kim Jae-in in the last five months and added: "If the parties on this termination issue are South Korea and not the US, the nuclear termination from the Korean peninsula will not find an impasse."
However, Ri's tone of speech differed dramatically compared to last year, as he told the U.S. General Assembly targeting the U.S. mainland with a North Korean rocket rocket was unavoidable after Trump's "Bad President" named Kim as a rocket man on a suicide mission.
This year at a United Nations conference, Trump, who last year threatened to "completely destruct" North Korea, praised Kim for his courage in taking steps to disamplement, but said a lot of work still needed to be done and sanctions had to remain until North Korea ended its nuclear program.
On Wednesday, Trump said he didn't have time for this, saying "If it takes two years, three years or five months - it doesn't matter."
China and Russia argued that the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for measures taken.
However, U.S. Foreign Secretary Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Security Council's "Enforcing the Security Council's sanctions must proceed vigorously and unfailed until we feel a complete, definitive and definitive nuclear termination."
The Security Council has unanimously increased sanctions on North Korea since 2006 in an effort to dismantle Pyongyang’s nuclear and ballistic missile programming.
Pompeo met Ri outside the UN General Assembly and said after that he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for the second summit.
Pompeo has visited North Korea three times this year, but its last tour has not gone smoothly.
He left Pyongyang in July saying that progress had been made, only for North Korea in a few hours later had condemned him for making "claims like gangsters."
North Korea pledged in a meeting with the Moon this month to expose the missile site and also the nuclear complex if the United States takes "appropriate measures."
He said Kim had told him the "equal measures" he was looking for was the security assurance that Trump had promised in Singapore and was heading towards a normal relationship with Washington.
Harvard students have taken courses to get enough rest
A new course at Harvard University this year has given all its students more asleep in an effort to combat an increasingly violent study culture through 'all-in-the-night' caffeine-based.
A teaching staff finds that students at the world's number one university are often unable to understand the basics of caring for themselves.
Charles Czeisler, professor of medical sleeps at Harvard Medical School and an expert at Brigham and Women's Hospital, provides a course she believes is the first of its kind in the United States.
He was inspired to start the course after giving a lecture on the effects of lack of sleep during learning.
‘At the end of the course, a girl came to me and said: ‘What’ is I just being told about this now, in my senior year?’’
He said no one had ever told her about the importance of sleeping - and that surprised me," he told The Telegraph.
This course was introduced for the first time this year, explaining to students the need for good sleep habits to assist academic and athletic performance, as well as improve their well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at the Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university's health services, said the university decided to introduce the course after discovering his students lacked sleep throughout the week.
The one-on-an-an-hour course involves several interactive tasks.
In one section, there is an image of a dormitory room, where students click on coffee cups, curtains, trainers and books to be told about the effects of caffeine and light and how the performance of the sport is affected by lack of sleep, and the importance of the bedding routine.
In other parts, participants were told how long-term sleep deficiency can increase the risk of heart attack, stroke, depression and cancer.
Campus map with interactive icons, then encouraging participants to think about their daily routine.
"We didn't change the behavior of students immediately.
But we believe they have the right to know - just like you have the right to know the health effects when choosing to smok," added Professor Czeisler.
Proud culture of ‘precaution all night’ still exists,” he said, adding that modern-day technology and increased stress on students meant a lack of sleeping is an escalating problem.
Ensuring that you have enough quality of sleep, it is supposed to be the 'secret weapon' of students to combat stress, exhaustion and anxiety, he says - even to avoid weight gain, because lack of sleep puts the brain into starvation mode, making it constantly hungry.
Raymond So, a California student who learns to study 19-year-old physical chemistry and biology, helped Professor Czeisler plan this course, after taking his classes last year during his first year at Harvard.
He said the course had opened his eyes and inspired him to expand the course throughout the campus.
The next step, he hopes, is to ask all postgraduate students to complete the same learning programme before joining competitive institutions.
Prof Czeisler suggested that students should consider the use of alarm clocks before and also after sleep, and to be aware of the harmful effects of 'blue-lighted' hazards released by electronic screens and LED lighting, which can damage your circadian rhythm from moving, causing sleep problems.
