Almanak: The Creator of Geiger Calculators
And now, the page of our "Sunday Morning": 30 September 1882, 136 years ago today, and COUNTING... the day in which future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger created a method of detecting and measuring radioactivity, a discovery that eventually led to a device called PengiraGeiger.
As the main basis of science since then, Geiger Counter became the backbone of pop culture, as in the 1950 film "Bells of Coronado", which starred unknown scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
Man: "What is it?"
Rogers: "It is Geiger Calculator, used to find radioactive minerals, such as uranium.
"When you're using these earphones, you'll be able to hear the atomic effects provided by radioactivity in minerals."
"Yes, it's read now."
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the creation that carries the name continues to last forever.
New cancer vaccines can teach the immune system to 'see’ bad cells
New cancer vaccines can teach the immune system to 'see’ nasty cells and kill them
Vaccination teaches the immune system to identify nasty cells as part of treatment
The affected method is to extract immune cells from the patient, altering it in the laboratory
It can 'see’ proteins that are common into many cancer cells and then reinvented
Trial vaccines show encouraging results to patients with different types of cancers.
A woman is treated with a vaccine, which teaches the immune system to get to recognize bad cells, recovering from her ovarian cancer after more than 18 months.
The method involves the extraction of immune cells from patients, altering it in the laboratory so that they can "see" a protein that is common to many types of cancer called HER2, and then re-inject the cells.
Professor Jay Berzofsky, from the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland, said: "Our test results show that we have a very good vaccine."
HER2 “causes the growth of several types of cancer,” “““including breast, ovarian, and colorectal cancers”.
The same approach to taking immune cells from patients and "teaching" their way to target cancer cells has been successful in treating types of leukemia.
