HIV/Aids: China reports a 14% increase in new cases
China has announced an increase of 14% for the number of people living with HIV and AIDS.
More than 820,000 people are infected in the country, health officials said.
About 40,000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018 alone.
Most new cases are transmitted through sex, signaling a change than before.
Normally, HIV spreads rapidly through parts of China due to a contaminated blood transfusion.
But the number of people with HIV this way has been reduced to almost zero, Chinese health officials said at a conference in Yunnan province.
However, year-on-the-year, the number of people with HIV and AIDS in China has increased by 100,000.
The dissemination of HIV through sexual intercourse is a serious issue in China's LGBT community.
Homosexuality is no longer a crime in China in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is said to was become increasingly prevalent.
Due to the country's conservative value, the study estimates that 70-90% of men who have sex with men will eventually marry women.
Most the spread of the disease is due to insufficient sexual protection in the relationship.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged universal access to HIV drugs as part of efforts to address the problem.
