Germany's new warship is delayed again
The German Navy should accept its latest frigate ship in 2014 to replace the Cold War warships, but ships will not be completed until next year due to increased system malfunctions and costs, according to local media reports.
“Rhineland-Pfalz” warshiper commissioning, the first ship under the brand of Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigat has now been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to Die Zeit’s newspaper citing a military spokesman.
This warship was supposed to join the Navy in 2014, unfortunately the post-handover problem hit the fate of this big project.
Four battleship Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigat ordered by the Navy in 2007 will replace the Bremen-class frigat.
It is understood that it will contain characteristics such as high-powered cannons, various anti-aircraft and anti-shipe equipment as well as some invisible features such as reducing radar detection, red lights and acaustic capabilities.
Another important feature is the longer duration of maintenance - this latest frigat must be able to sail for up to two years from its base.
However, various barriers mean this sophisticated warshiper who is said to be capable of highlighting Germany's power abroad - would be outdated by the time this ship joined the Navy's service, according to Die Zeit
The unfortunate F125 Frigat made headlines last year, when German Navy officials refused to commission the warships and returned it to the Blohm & Voss shipbuilding company in Hamburg.
This is the first time the Navy has returned the ship back to its builder after delivery.
Not much is known why ships were returned, but the German newspaper mentioned some “software and devices” damage made this warship useless if sent for combat missions.
The damage to the software should be cared as the Baden-Wuerttemberg-class warships will be operated by only 120 sailors - half of which is required to operate the old Bremen-class frigat.
There is also a time when these warships are too heavy and this will reduce performance and limit the Navy's ability to make any improvements in the future.
“Rheinland-Pfalz” ““Rheinland-Pfalz”” ““Rheinland-Pfalz” is believed to be twice heavier than the same-class warship used by German forces during the Second World War.
Apart from damage to the device, the overall price of the project - including training for sailors - is also an issue.
The project is said to have reached €3.1 billion ($3.6bn) — increased from its original €2.2 billion price.
The problems facing this latest frigate warship show how lagging the German naval capabilities are.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, the head of the defense committee in parliament, acknowledged that the Navy was facing a shortage of capable warships.
Officials also said the issue worsened, as old warships that had been discontinued did not get replacement warships.
He lamented that none of Baden-Wurettemberg-class frigat warships could join the Navy.
The National Trust Keeps The Life Of Bats
A recent study being conducted at an estate in Scotland's highland aims to explain why bats use the terrain to hunt for food.
It is hoped that this study will lead to a recent discovery on the behavior of unique mammals who are able to fly fly and can serve as a guide for conservation activities.
A study by scientists at the National Trust for Scotland will follow common bats, and soprano pipistrelles bats as well as long-earned chocolate bats and Daubenton's bates at Inverewe Park in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be placed in certain locations around the property to track bat activities throughout the season.
NHS employees and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using a mobile tracking device.
Experts analyze the sounds of all recordings will identify the frequency of each type of bat and its species and acts of this bat.
A map of habitat and reports will be produced to create an scalable earth-shaped picture regarding the behavior of bats.
Rob Dewar, a conservation advisor to the NTS, hopes the results of this study will show the most important habitat area to bats and how it is used by every species of bat.
This information will help to identify habitat management benefits such as making grazing fields and the best way to maintain forests for bats and other related species.
The total number of bats in Scotland and the UK has decreased significantly over the past century.
Bats are threatened by buildings and developmental works that interfere with bat sites perching and habitat loss.
Wind turbines and lamps are also a risk to bats similar to fly trap papers and some chemicals used to treat building materials as well as pet cat attacks.
It's actually not blind.
Due to the hunting bat at night, their ears are more useful than their eyes.
They use location detection techniques using sounds to identify the position of insects and barriers while in flight.
The NTS, which is responsible for safeguarding more than 270 historical buildings, 38 important parks and 76,000 hectares of land all over the place in the country.
They have ten trained specialists who often conduct surveys, examine places of diment and sometimes save bats.
The organization has also established Scotland's first bat shelter in Threave estets in Dumfries and Galloway, where eight out of ten bat species in Scotland live.
David Thompson estate manager said this estate is the most suitable place for bats.
“In Thereave we have a very suitable place for bats,” he said.
“We have old buildings, a lot of large trees and suitable habitat.
There’s much more we don’t know about bats, so the work we’re carrying out here and elsewhere will help us understand what bats need to live and grow.
He emphasized how important it is to carry out inspections before starting maintenance work in real estate as there is an accidental possibility that we can destroy the nests of bats that will contain bat children in which this is capable of killing 400 female and bat bat bats, and may destroy the entire population of local bats.
Bats are protected and is an offence to kill, persecut or harash or destroy a nested bat place.
Elisabeth Ferrell, an official for Bat Conservation Trust encourages people to help.
He said: “There’s still a lot of things we have to learn about bats and for many species we don’t know how their population is growing.” “
