Overseas buyers will be subject to higher stamp duty when they purchase a property in the UK
Foreign buyers will be subjected to higher stamp duty when they purchase properties in the UK and the excess money will be used to help homelessness under Tory’s latest plan.”
This move will eliminate Corbyn's success to attract young voters
This stamp duty will be imposed on those who do not pay taxes in the UK.
Treasury is expected to collect up to £120 million a year - to help homeless
Foreign buyers will be subjected to higher stamp duty when they buy real estate in the U.K. - money from this will be used to help homelessness, Theresa May said today.
This move seems to dismantle Jeremy Corbyn's success to attract young voters with the promise to provide affordable housing and make high-income people a target.
This stamp duty increase will be imposed on individuals and firms who do not pay taxes in the UK, money from this revenue will help the government overcome the problems of homeless people.
This add-on is an add-on stamp duty that has already been imposed including higher duties that have been introduced two years ago on a second house and houses purchased for rent that may be charged three per cent.
The Treasury is expected to collect up to £120 million a year through this move.
An estimated 13 percent of London's new properties were bought by non-UK citizens, causing prices to soar and makes it difficult for those who want to buy their first home.
Most affluent areas – especially in the nation’s capital — have become “silent silence” because many of its owners are foreign owners who spend a lot of time abroad.
This new policy has just been unveiled a few weeks after Boris Johnson is demanding that stamp duty be reduced so that more young people can afford to buy their first home.
He accused large construction companies of deliberately maintaining high property prices by buying land but not developing them, and urging Mrs May to eliminate quotas on affordable homes to overcome Britain’s “amish housing problems””.
