The ideal time to drink beer is 5:30pm on Saturday, according to a study
This summer's heaviour brings good luck to British pubs who are in trouble, but network restaurants are under stress.
Groups of pubs and bars showed 2.7 per cent sales increase in July - but in restaurants it fell 4.8 percent.
Peter Martin of the CGA’s business consulting firm, said: “Continuous hot weather and a longer England World Cup’s campaign means that the month of July will follow the pattern of June, where pubs rise 2.8 per cent, unless restaurants decline worse.
The 1.8 percent drop in restaurants in June worsened in July.
The performance of a pub that prioritizes drinks and bars is very strong compared to declining restaurants.
The food-precising pub also suffers bad luck due to the hot weather, but it's not as bad as restaurant operators.
It seems that most people just want to go out for a beer.
Pubs and bars recorded an increase in sales of 6.6 per cent for the month, while food fell 3 percent.
Paul Newman’s entertainment and food analyst from RSM said: “This decision appears to remain the same and continues since the end of April.
Weather and social events or sporting events are still a major factor in the increase in sales in markets outdoors.
