Earthquakes and earthquakes in Indonesia
At least 384 have been killed by a strong earthquake and tsunami that hit the city of Palu in Indonesia on Friday, according to authorities, the death toll is expected to rise.
In the absence of a communication system, the authorities were unable to obtain information from Donggala's government, the northern region of Palu near the epicentre of the quake with a magnitude of 7.5-magnical tremors.
In Palu more than 16,000 people were evacuated after the disaster.
Here are the main facts about Palu and Donggala, on the island of Sulawesi:
Palu is the capital of Central Sulawesi province, located at the end of the narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi Island, with a population of 379,800 in 2017.
The city is celebrating its 40th anniversary when the earthquake and tsunami hit.
Donggala is a 300 km (180-mile) north-seash coast of Sulawesi Island.
The area, the administrative region under the region, had a population of 299,200 as of 2017.
Fish and animal husbandry have become the economic backbone of the Central Sulawesi region, especially in Donggala's waters.
Nickel mining is also important in the region, but is concentrated only in Morowali, on the beach opposite Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been hit by Tsumani several times in 100 years, according to Indonesia's Disaster Mitigation Agency.
In 1938, the tsunami killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of homes in Donggala.
The tsunami also struck western Donggala in 1996, killing nine people.
Indonesia is seismically located on the Pacific Ring of Fire and has always been hit by earthquakes.
Here are major earthquakes and tsunami of the last few years:
2004: A major earthquake on the west-west Acheh province of Indonesia in northern Sumatra on Dec 26 has triggered a tsunami that struck 14 countries, killing 226,000 people along the waters of the Indian Ocean, half of them in Aceh.
2005: A series of earthquakes hit the west coast of Sumatra in late March and early April.
Hundreds were killed on Nias Island on the coast of Sumatra.
2006: An 6.8-magnitude quake struck southern Java, Indonesia's most populous island, triggering a tsunami that hit the southern coast, killing nearly 700 people.
2009: A magnitude 7.6 quake struck close to Padang City, the provincial capital of West Sumatra.
More than 1,100 people were killed.
2010: An 7.5-magnitude Earthquake struck one of the Mentawai Islands, in Sumatra, triggering a 10-metre tsunami that destroyed dozens of villages and killed 300 people.
2016: A temperate quake struck Pidie Jaya's Acheh-ruled area, causing destruction and panic, reminiscent of the population of a major devacation of earthquakes and tsunamis in 2004.
No tsunami was triggered at the time, but more than 100 were killed due to the collapse of buildings.
2018: A strong earthquake struck the Indonesian Lombok tourist island, killing more than 500 people, especially in the northern part of the island.
The quake destroyed thousands of buildings and left thousands of tourists strandened at the time.
