Public prosecutors demand extraordinary death penalty for NYC terror attack suspects
The New York prosecutor is demanding the death penalty for Sayfullo Saipov, a New York City suspected of a terror attack that kills eight people - an extraordinary sentence that is no longer carried out in the state mentioned for federal crimes since 1953.
Saipov, 30, is accused of using a Home Depot rental truck to carry out an attack on a bicycle path along the Western-Sided Highway in Lower Manhattan, crashing into pedestrians and cyclists on the Oct route.
To justify the death penalty, prosecutors need to prove that Saipov "intends" the killing of the eight victims and "intentions" of inflicting serious bodily harm, according to a notice with the intention of demanding the death penalty, filed in Southern District, New York.
Both charges led to the death penalty, according to the court's documents.
A few weeks after the attack, a federal grand jury has charged Saipov with 22 counts including eight counts of murder due to illegal activities, which have often been worn by public prosecutors in organized criminal cases, and an accusation of terrorism and destroying motor vehicles.
The attack requires “meticulous planning and planning””, according to the prosecutor, describes how Saipov carried out the action as “marturned, cruel and humiliation.”
"Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov has caused injury, pain, and loss to the family and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza, and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco," according to noticing.
Five of the victims were tourists from Argentina.
It's been a long time that the Southern District of New York has claimed the death penalty.
The defendant, Khalid Barnes, was charged with the murder of two drug traffickers but was finally sentenced to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The last sentencation to the New York federal case was in 1953 for Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a married couple who had been sentenced after they were sentenced to espions for the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years earlier.
Both Rosenberg were executed with an electric seat on June 19, 1953.
Saipov, an Uzbek national, did not feel guilty on the day and month after the attack according to court documents.
He made a statement to investigators that he was happy with what he did, according to the police.
Saipov said he was tempted to carry out an attack after seeing an ISIS video on the phone, according to allegations.
He also asked to display an ISIS flag in the hospital's room, according to the police.
He pleads not guilty to 22 charges.
David Patton, one of the federal advocates representing Saipov, said they were "really disappointed" with the prosecutor's decision.
"We feel the decision to claim the death penalty compared to accepting a guilty plea for a life imprisonment with no possibility to be released, merely prolonging the trauma of this event for all those involved," Patton said.
Saipov's defence had earlier asked the prosecution not to demand the death penalty.
